Z||000000||85Josh||08-05-2001||01:44 PM||||Ok, I thought I was releativly knowledgeable about cars, but maybe not! Anyway, I am starting the clutch replacement prcedure outlined on the Fiero Factory website, and when I jacked the car up, just happened to notice that when I turn one wheel, the other dosen't turn. I am wondering if this has anything to do with the fact that the clutch relase bearing is stuck, which is why I'm changing the clutch. TIA<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||5
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-05-2001||02:54 PM||||I dont think so. I am not real familiar with the transaxels. But I think if you have aposi rearend or diferental then the other wheel will turn in the same direction while your doing what you describe.I f you have open diferental like I think fireos have you will sometimes have what you are describing or where one wheel rotates in the other direction.Now I'll bet you kinda knew this already.so I dont know if this helps you in any way.good luck||||reg||4
Z||000002||JayUSA77||08-05-2001||03:14 PM||||Not good for YOU!!!!! when i changed my clutch both sides turned. or at least i remembered it did. You might have gears that are broke. When you take off your halfshafts you can look in there and see all the gears. See if you can see loose gears. Thats my advice<BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||08-05-2001||03:35 PM||||Jay, that's kinda what I'm afraid of. I guess we'll see soon, I'm trying to get parts loose now. Will kep ya'll informed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-06-2001||09:19 AM||||If the clutch is engaged and the trans is in gear (engine not running) then when you turn one wheel the other will turn in the other direction at the same speed.  If you hold one wheel you will not be able to turn the other.<P>If the trans is in neutral then the same could happen or one wheel will stay still while the other wneel and the trans input shaft both turn.<P>Our cars don't have posi or limited slip differentials so you shouldn't get both wheels turning in the same direction if the trans is in gear, clutch engaged.<P>Actually, if you had posi and the trans was in gear with the clutch engaged (engine not running) you wouldn't be able to turn either wheel unless you had absolutely no compression.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||08-06-2001||05:25 PM||||What Carrolles said...  I think your trans must have been in neutral.  Otherwise your car would not move when driving it.  Instead of the engery being transmitted thru the differential and out to the other wheel, it was spinning the trans output shaft but the trans was in neutral.||||reg||1
Z||000006||85Josh||08-06-2001||08:37 PM||||Actually, the clutch release bearing is stuck in the disengaged position, which is why I am fooling with it at all. Today, I got home late, and got all the parts loose, and all the bellhousing bolts loose, except the one in the rear at the bottom. I'll bust it loose tommorrow evening, hopefully. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||85Josh||08-08-2001||09:02 PM||||Well, it turns out that the clutch disk has broken in pieces. The tranny checks out ok, thank God. Thanks for the replies, fellas.||||reg||1
